= Category =

== Overview ==
A '''category''' is a grouping or kind used to distinguish entities, concepts or phenomena.

In ordinary classification, categories organize items according to shared characteristics or purpose.

In philosophy, "categories" can mean the most general kinds of beings or concepts.

The word therefore ranges from practical grouping to deep ontological structure.

== Philosophical categories ==
Aristotle proposed a famous system of highest categories including substance, quantity, quality and relation.

Later philosophical systems developed very different category schemes.

Some treat categories as kinds that exist in reality.

Others treat them as structures of thought or language.

This disagreement mirrors a general question in [[Ontology|ontology]]: are our categories discovered in the world or constructed as useful ways of representing it?

== Practical categories ==
A practical category groups things for a purpose.

Examples:
* books by subject;
* organisms by taxonomic group;
* support tickets by issue type;
* products by department.

A category can be useful even if it does not correspond to a natural kind.

Purpose-built categories often trade philosophical depth for usability.

== Categories and concepts ==
A [[Concept]] supplies a structured meaning.

A category groups or classifies items.

In many systems the two align closely, but they should not be identified automatically.

A concept can have vague boundaries; a database category may require a binary membership decision.

== Categories and classes ==
Formal systems commonly use '''class''' for a mathematically or logically defined grouping of individuals.

"Category" is broader.

A category can be:
* informal;
* social;
* fuzzy;
* navigational;
* formal.

A class is usually defined within a particular formalism.

== Category membership ==
Membership can be based on:

* necessary and sufficient conditions;
* prototypes;
* similarity;
* expert judgment;
* rules;
* statistical classification;
* institutional convention.

Different membership rules can produce different boundaries even when labels match.

== Hierarchy ==
Categories can be organized hierarchically.

For example:

<pre>
animal
 └→ mammal
    └→ whale
</pre>

But the meaning of parent–child edges must be stated.

A hierarchy can mix:
* subclass;
* part–whole;
* topic nesting;
* organizational containment.

Such mixing makes automated reasoning unsafe.

== Polyhierarchy ==
A category can have more than one parent.

For example, a topic may belong to both "Computer science" and "Mathematics."

Polyhierarchy can reflect legitimate multiple perspectives.

It also means the structure is a directed graph rather than a simple tree.

== Category mistakes ==
A '''category mistake''' attributes something of the wrong logical or conceptual kind.

For example, asking what color an abstract theorem is confuses categories of property.

Category distinctions therefore help prevent malformed questions and representations.

== MediaWiki categories ==
MediaWiki Category pages are primarily organizational/navigation structures.

They can group pages automatically and form category hierarchies.

A MediaWiki category is not automatically a philosophical category, ontology class or logical type.

Its semantic role depends on how the wiki defines it.

== BITwiki application ==
BITwiki uses categories for domains and navigation while keeping entity type and ontology separate.

This is an implementation choice intended to avoid conflating "what kind of thing is this?" with "where should a reader browse it?"

